# Molecular fragments (substituents) used by the GUI builder to
# build structures by attaching groups to an existing molecule.
#
# The format is the same as in the crystal and molecule libraries,
# with four additional keywords after the endmolecule line:
#
#   anchor <i> ..... the atom that attaches to the rest of the structure
#   attach <j> ..... the placeholder atom marking the direction of the
#                    attachment bond. If it is a real atom (a hydrogen)
#                    it caps the anchor when the fragment is placed on
#                    empty space; if it is a dummy (bq, for the dative
#                    ligands) only its direction is used. In both cases
#                    it is removed when the fragment bonds to an atom.
#   category <str> . the submenu the fragment is listed under. A category of
#                    the form "parent/child" nests it one level deeper
#   radius <r> ..... fictitious covalent radius, in angstrom. Its presence
#                    marks the fragment as a LIGAND: the whole fragment is
#                    placed like a single atom of that radius, which BONDS
#                    TO the clicked atom instead of replacing it. Here the
#                    anchor is the donor nearest the metal and attach is a
#                    bq sitting at the metal position, so the anchor lands
#                    at atmcov(clicked) + radius from it. The rotation is
#                    free about that axis and the metal lies on it, so the
#                    remaining donors keep their distances by themselves.
#
# Only the DIRECTION of the anchor-to-attach vector is used: the bond
# distance is recomputed from the covalent radii (plus radius, for a
# ligand) at attachment time. Coordinates are in angstrom.
#
# Most of these fragments were converted from the Avogadro / Open
# Chemistry fragment library, https://github.com/OpenChemistry/fragments.
